          "reasoning_content": "We need to extract unverified occupational, executive, ownership, founder, board or trustee role proposals.\n\nFrom entries:\n\nEntry0: \"Inez Rao met Paolo Wu at Tamarack Power.\" No role claim.\n\nEntry1: \"The notice names Inez Rao as secretary of Ash Works; it gives no employer or role for Paolo Wu.\"\n\nSo role claim: Inez Rao as secretary of Ash Works. Secretary is a role. Is it executive? Possibly but taxonomy: role_kind can be employee, executive, board_director, etc. Secretary could be an employee (or executive) but not clearly. We need decide: \"secretary\" generic could be employee; not necessarily board. So role_kind = employee (or unknown). It is a role, not correction. For Paolo Wu, there is no role, so nothing.\n\nThus we have one interpretation.\n\nWe need selections: we need a selection for the role title \"secretary\". Also selections for subject name \"Inez Rao\" and organization \"Ash Works\". Provide entry references and token ids.\n\nIn entry1 tokens: I need token IDs for \"secretary\" token id 6. Organization \"Ash Works\" tokens 8-9? token8 is \"Ash\", token9 is \"Works\". So organization selection from 8 to9 inclusive.\n\nSubject \"Inez Rao\": tokens 3 and4. So selection id for that.\n\nWe need a Go-generated context selection per referenced entry? The spec: \"one Go-generated context selection per referenced entry must total at most 64.\" Not sure we need a context selection; maybe used for context_links. Not necessary if no links.\n\nNow build grounding:\n\n- selections: need unique ids not using name IDs or ctx_ prefix. Use s0, s1, s2 etc.\n\nSelect role title: selection id \"s0\", entry 1, first_token 6, last_token 6.\n\nSelect subject: \"s1\", entry1, first_token 3, last_token 4.\n\nSelect organization: \"s2\", entry1, first_token 8, last_token 9.\n\nInterpretations: one.\n\nInterpretation fields:\n\nid: i0\n\nstatement_entry: 1\n\nfocus: selection id for role title → \"s0\"\n\nkind: \"role\"\n\nnormalized: maybe \"secretary\" (short label). Provide.\n\nrole_kind: we need decide. I'd choose \"employee\". Since it's secretary of the organization, likely an employee. Not board. Use \"employee\".\n\nsubjects: list of person mention IDs: from names list, Inez Rao is n0. So subjects: [\"n0\"].\n\norganizations: organization mention IDs: Ash Works is n3.\n\nsubject_surface: \"s1\"\n\norganization_surface: \"s2\"\n\nadditional_evidence: empty list [] (no extra).\n\nbinding: we need assess literal match. The subject_surface text \"Inez Rao\" matches the name mention text \"Inez Rao\"? Name candidate n0 text is \"Inez Rao\". The token selection s1 covers tokens \"Inez Rao\" exactly same text. So binding can be \"direct\". Organization_surface \"Ash Works\" matches n3 text \"Ash Works\". So binding also direct. Since binding expects object with person and organization? spec: grounding.binding is direct only when both selected surfaces exactly match chosen candidates' text. So we can set binding: \"direct\". (maybe as string). \n\nstatus: \"asserted\" (since the notice names her). No negation.\n\nNow referents: one record for the role interpretation.\n\nInterpretation: i0.\n\nsubject: object with state and candidates.\n\nWe need to assess. The claim: Inez Rao as secretary of Ash Works. This is unverified occupational role; we need to assess referent. The subject referent: the claim is that person entity is Inez Rao; we have name candidate n0, matches. We can mark state as \"proposed\" with candidate n0. Similarly organization: state \"proposed\" with candidate n3.\n\nThus:\n\nreferents: [{ \"interpretation\":\"i0\", \"subject\": {\"state\":\"proposed\",\"candidates\":[\"n0\"]}, \"organization\": {\"state\":\"proposed\",\"candidates\":[\"n3\"]} }]\n\nNo corrections. No context_links.\n\nNow produce JSON with top-level fields: grounding, interpretations? Actually spec: top-level fields: grounding, referents, corrections. Also context_links. It says Always emit all three top-level fields and non-null arrays, including empty ones. So we need grounding (contains selections), interpretations (maybe part of grounding? The spec earlier defines grounding contains selections, interpretations and context_links? Actually reading: \"grounding contains selections, interpretations and context_links.\" So grounding must include selections array, interpretations array, context_links array. Then separate referents and corrections arrays.\n\nThus structure:\n\n{\n \"grounding\": {\n   \"selections\": [...],\n   \"interpretations\": [...],\n   \"context_links\": []\n },\n \"referents\": [...],\n \"corrections\": []\n}\n\nNow fill selections: each with id, entry, first_token, last_token.\n\nInterpretations: each with required fields: id, statement_entry, focus, kind, normalized, role_kind, subjects, organizations, subject_surface, organization_surface, additional_evidence, binding, status.\n\nMake sure all fields present.\n\nNow produce final answer."
